  "salt": "kYt0fvgpxXhOSqHq5tsbhA==",
  "text": "The Celts divided the seasons by a cosmological scheme. They did not begin and end seasons at solstices and equinoxes as most ancient peoples did. Instead they placed the season points midway between those solar pivots. Winter began at Samhain on November 1. Spring began at Imbolc on February 1. Summer began at Beltane on May 1. Fall began at Lughnasa on August 1. Some scholars argue this division was Irish rather than Celtic. The scheme does not appear among continental Celts and fits the seasons of Ireland better than those of France. Monuments such as Stonehenge and Newgrange predate the Celts. They were raised to chart the stations of the solar year, not the midpoints the Celts kept. Yet the Celts adopted them and wrapped them in myth. The Celts also held that darkness precedes light in each day and each year. Thus Samhain opened on the evening of October 31, now called Hallowe'en. Samhain ended one year and began the next.",
